    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black text-white tracking-tight mb-6">
      Caddy Server JSON Access Log Grok Patterns &amp; Ingestion Architectures
    </h1>

    <div class="bg-emerald-950/20 border-l-4 border-l-emerald-500 border-y border-r border-emerald-500/30 rounded-xl p-6 shadow-xl mb-10">
      <div class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 font-mono mb-2">
        ⚡ Quick Answer: Parsing Caddy 2 JSON Logs
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed">
        Unlike NGINX which defaults to combined text strings, <strong>Caddy 2 emits structured JSON logs by default</strong>. While standard Grok regex can parse JSON via nested key extractions, modern pipelines (Vector, Fluent Bit, or Logstash) process Caddy logs 10x faster using native JSON decoders, extracting fields like <code>ts</code> (float epoch), <code>duration</code> (float seconds), and <code>request.client_ip</code> directly into search indices.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1: Caddy 2 Logging Architecture -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Caddy 2 Structured Logging: Why JSON Replaced CLF</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Traditional web servers (Apache, NGINX, HAProxy) rely on string-interpolated access logs formatted in Common Log Format (CLF) or Combined Log Format. While human-readable in terminal pagers, text logs break catastrophically when request URIs or User-Agent headers contain unescaped quotes, brackets, or newline characters.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Caddy 2 addresses this by utilizing the high-performance Uber <code>zap</code> logging library. Every HTTP request is serialized as a single-line JSON object with strongly typed attributes:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/90 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-cyan-300 overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <pre is:raw>
{
  "level": "info",
  "ts": 1757343120.412891,
  "logger": "http.log.access.log0",
  "msg": "handled request",
  "request": {
    "remote_ip": "203.0.113.195",
    "remote_port": "51240",
    "client_ip": "203.0.113.195",
    "proto": "HTTP/2.0",
    "method": "POST",
    "host": "api.cloudstack.io",
    "uri": "/v1/telemetry/ingest?batch=true",
    "headers": {
      "User-Agent": ["Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_15_7)..."],
      "Accept": ["application/json"],
      "X-Forwarded-For": ["203.0.113.195"]
    },
    "tls": {
      "resumed": false,
      "version": 772,
      "cipher_suite": 4865,
      "proto": "h2",
      "server_name": "api.cloudstack.io"
    }
  },
  "bytes_read": 4096,
  "user_id": "",
  "duration": 0.0142851,
  "size": 1840,
  "status": 200,
  "resp_headers": {
    "Content-Type": ["application/json; charset=utf-8"],
    "Server": ["Caddy"]
  }
}
        </pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: Grok Pattern Definition -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Legacy Ingestion: Grok Regular Expression Pattern</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        If you are ingesting Caddy logs into legacy Logstash, Graylog, or AWS CloudWatch filter patterns that enforce Grok regex parsing, use the following pattern definition:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/90 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-emerald-400 overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <pre is:raw>
# Custom Grok Pattern for Caddy 2 Access Logs
CADDY_TS (?:%{NUMBER:timestamp_epoch})
CADDY_DURATION (?:%{NUMBER:request_duration_sec:float})
CADDY_JSON ^\{"level":"%{WORD:log_level}","ts":%{CADDY_TS},"logger":"%{DATA:logger}","msg":"%{DATA:message}","request":\{"remote_ip":"%{IP:remote_ip}","remote_port":"%{POSINT:remote_port}","client_ip":"%{IP:client_ip}","proto":"%{DATA:http_protocol}","method":"%{WORD:http_method}","host":"%{HOSTNAME:http_host}","uri":"%{NOTSPACE:request_uri}".*?"duration":%{CADDY_DURATION},"size":%{POSINT:response_bytes:int},"status":%{POSINT:http_status_code:int}
        </pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-400 text-xs font-mono mb-4">
        *Performance Note: Regex backtracking over nested JSON strings costs ~15-25 microseconds per log line on modern Intel Xeon cores. For fleets generating >5,000 requests/sec, Grok consumes significant CPU.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: Modern High-Throughput Ingestion Pipelines -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">High-Throughput Ingestion: Vector &amp; Fluent Bit Pipelines</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Modern observability stacks bypass Grok regular expressions entirely by using native SIMD-accelerated JSON parsers. Below are production configurations for Vector (Datadog) and Fluent Bit:
      </p>

      <!-- Vector VRL -->
      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-400 font-mono mb-2">Vector Remap Language (VRL) Transform:</h3>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/90 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-slate-200 overflow-x-auto">
          <pre is:raw>
[transforms.caddy_parser]
type = "remap"
inputs = ["caddy_file_source"]
source = '''
  # Parse raw JSON string
  . = parse_json!(.message)

  # Normalize standard telemetry schema
  .timestamp = from_unix_timestamp!(to_int!(.ts), unit: "seconds")
  .client_ip = .request.client_ip
  .http_method = .request.method
  .http_host = .request.host
  .http_path = .request.uri
  .http_status = to_int!(.status)
  .response_time_ms = .duration * 1000.0
  .response_bytes = to_int!(.size)

  # Categorize HTTP status class
  if .http_status >= 500 {
    .status_class = "5xx_server_error"
  } else if .http_status >= 400 {
    .status_class = "4xx_client_error"
  } else {
    .status_class = "success"
  }

  # Clean unneeded raw fields
  del(.logger)
  del(.msg)
'''
          </pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Fluent Bit -->
      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-400 font-mono mb-2">Fluent Bit Parser Config (parsers.conf):</h3>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/90 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-slate-200 overflow-x-auto">
          <pre is:raw>
[PARSER]
    Name        caddy_json
    Format      json
    Time_Key    ts
    Time_Format %s
    Time_Keep   On
    # Types maps numeric floats and integers automatically
    Types duration:float status:integer size:integer
          </pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: Caddyfile Configuration Best Practices -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Production Caddyfile Logging Directive</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
        To configure rolling access logs with automated gzip compression and trusted proxy IP extraction, configure your <code>Caddyfile</code> as follows:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/90 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-amber-300 overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <pre is:raw>
api.example.com {
    # Extract authentic client IP behind Cloudflare or AWS ALB
    trusted_proxies 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 10.0.0.0/8

    log {
        output file /var/log/caddy/access.log {
            roll_size 100mb
            roll_keep 10
            roll_keep_for 720h
            roll_gzip
        }
        format json {
            time_format "epoch"
            time_key "ts"
        }
    }

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
        </pre>
      </div>
    </section>

